{
  "order": 5,
  "title": "Film Projector",
  "hook": "Eighteen still pictures a second, each held dead still in front of the lens, then whisked away.",
  "category": "optics",
  "summary": [
    "A film projector turns a strip of still photographs into motion. A claw pulls the film down one frame and holds it still in the gate while a bright lamp shines through it and the lens throws the picture onto the screen, then it pulls down the next.",
    "A spinning shutter blocks the light while each frame moves, so you only ever see stills. At 18 frames a second your eye blends them into continuous motion. The front reel feeds the film in; the rear reel winds it back up."
  ],
